# WHERE the user's `is_ok` comes from must not change whether it is CALLED.
#
# METHOD PROVENANCE is the axis that killed three successive attempts at fixing
# `t1017` with a receiver-keyed lookup table. A table keyed on the direct
# `{Type}__{method}` spelling cannot see a method that arrives through a trait
# impl (registered as `{Trait}_for_{Type}__{method}`), through a trait DEFAULT,
# or through a trait BOUND on a generic parameter — where the method lives on
# the bound and belongs to no concrete type at all. Each of those looked like
# "the type has no such method" and each would have REJECTED a healthy program.
# The shipped fix has no table: the tag check is gated on the typed
# `is_option_or_result` predicate, and every other receiver falls through to the
# ordinary dispatch, which already knows all four provenances.
#
# Every cell below printed `false` before the fix — the dispatch gate folded the
# call to `Constant::Bool(false)` before provenance was ever consulted — and
# prints `true` after.
#
#   bare equip                       true
#   self-receiver, from another
#     method of the same equip block true
#   trait impl                       true
#   trait default                    true
#   trait-bound generic parameter    true
#   Box[Trait] (trait object)        true
#
# ⚠ TWO CELLS OF THIS AXIS ARE COVERED ELSEWHERE, and neither is an omission:
#   * `Box[UserStruct]` — the RUST lane fixes it exactly like the rest (`false`
#     → `true`), but the SELF-HOST lane cannot build ANY method call on a
#     `Box[UserStruct]`, four-name or not: it mangles `Box__<Inner>__<method>`
#     and link-fails. Keeping the cell here would enrol a self-host non-MATCH
#     into `runtime_parity_corpus`, which the round's own inflow may not do, so
#     it lives in `known_gaps/sh_box_user_struct_method_link_error.gg` with the
#     self-host gap filed (`todo/t1020`).
#   * CROSS-MODULE `equip` — a different resolution avenue
#     (`resolve_method_by_name`, keyed on the type NAME because TypeIds do not
#     match across modules), so it needs a multi-file fixture:
#     `user_four_name_xmod_equip/`.
#
# ⚠ THE TRAIT-PROVENANCE BODIES ARE MULTI-LINE ON PURPOSE, and it is not style.
# `todo/t1018` is a live, independent defect: a trait-implementing `equip`
# method whose body is a SINGLE-LINE `return <expr>` discards its return value
# (`_0 = const unit`), while the identical body in a BARE equip block is
# correct. A single-line trait cell here would print `false` for t1018's reason,
# not for this fixture's, and would read as a failure of the fix under test.
# Restore the natural single-line spelling when t1018 lands.
trait Checkable:
    bool is_ok(self)

trait Defaulted:
    bool is_ok(self):
        int marker = 1
        return marker > 0

struct BareBox:
    int v

equip BareBox:
    bool is_ok(self): return self.v > 0
    # The SELF-RECEIVER cell: an `is_ok` reached as `self.is_ok()` from inside
    # another method of the same block, not from a local at the call site.
    bool double_check(self): return self.is_ok()

struct Sensor:
    int v

equip Sensor with Checkable:
    bool is_ok(self):
        int t = self.v
        return t > 0

struct Fallback:
    int v

equip Fallback with Defaulted:
    pass

trait Shifter:
    bool is_ok(self)

struct Boxed:
    int v

equip Boxed with Shifter:
    bool is_ok(self):
        int t = self.v
        return t > 0

bool probe[Checkable T](T a):
    return a.is_ok()

void main():
    BareBox b = BareBox(5)
    print(b.is_ok())
    print(b.double_check())

    Sensor s = Sensor(5)
    print(s.is_ok())

    Fallback f = Fallback(5)
    print(f.is_ok())

    print(probe[Sensor](s))

    Box[Shifter] t = Box.new(Boxed(5))
    print(t.is_ok())
